## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a beginner-friendly Azure data pipeline that handles schema drift, produces a dimensional model, and captures lineage. It also probes how to isolate raw data, apply transformations, and enforce simple data quality gates with observable provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Schema drift handling in Parquet
- Dimensional modeling (DimUsers, FactEvents)
- Drift capture via JSON blob
- Data quality checks (NOT NULL, timestamp parse)
- Data lineage with Purview integration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: drift handling in a dataflow step
function transform(record) {
  const known = { user_id, event_time, event_type };
  const drift = _.omit(record, Object.keys(known));
  return { ...record, drift_json: JSON.stringify(drift) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift handling with evolving schemas?
- How would you extend this to handle very high throughput volumes?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical design of data contracts, lineage, and governance in a multi-domain Azure data mesh, with real ingestion patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Data contracts across domains
- End-to-end lineage with Purview
- Delta Lake schema evolution and time travel
- Domain isolation via access controls
- Canary tests for contract changes

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo contract test for incoming record
function validateContract(record, contract) {
  // contract = {schema: {...}, required: [...], checks: [...]}
  if (!contract.required.every(k => k in record)) return false;
  // simple type checks
  for (let key of Object.keys(contract.schema)) {
    if (typeof record[key] !== contract.schema[key]) return false;
  }
  // custom checks
  return contract.checks.every(fn => fn(record));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you version contracts and rollback a breaking change?
- How would you monitor contract violations and auto-generate lineage dashboards?

